From: SP11 audio reverse-engineering <sp11@local>
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: lpass-va-macro: SP11 match Windows DMIC divider

Native Windows on Microsoft Denali (Surface Pro 11) votes the VA-path
clock at 19.2 MHz and programs CDC_VA_TOP_CSR_DMIC0_CTL[3:1] to 2,
which is the VA macro DIV4 selector.

Linux already requests the VA MCLK at 2 * VA_MACRO_MCLK_FREQ (19.2 MHz),
but va_macro_validate_dmic_sample_rate() calculates the divider from only
VA_MACRO_MCLK_FREQ (9.6 MHz).  With Denali's 4.8 MHz DT request this picks
DIV2/selector 0 instead of Windows DIV4/selector 2.

Use the actual 19.2 MHz VA MCLK as the divider basis on Microsoft Denali.
Keep the DT request at 4.8 MHz and leave all other machines unchanged.

Signed-off-by: SP11 audio reverse-engineering <sp11@local>
---
 sound/soc/codecs/lpass-va-macro.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/sound/soc/codecs/lpass-va-macro.c b/sound/soc/codecs/lpass-va-macro.c
--- a/sound/soc/codecs/lpass-va-macro.c
+++ b/sound/soc/codecs/lpass-va-macro.c
@@ -7,6 +7,7 @@
 #include <linux/init.h>
 #include <linux/io.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
+#include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/of_clk.h>
 #include <linux/of_platform.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
@@ -1433,6 +1434,14 @@ static int va_macro_validate_dmic_sample_rate(u32 dmic_sample_rate,
 	u32 div_factor;
 	u32 mclk_rate = VA_MACRO_MCLK_FREQ;

+	/*
+	 * Native Windows on Surface Pro 11 (Denali) votes the VA MCLK at
+	 * 19.2 MHz and programs the DMIC clock selector from that rate.
+	 * Linux already requests that same 19.2 MHz MCLK in probe.
+	 */
+	if (of_machine_is_compatible("microsoft,denali"))
+		mclk_rate = 2 * VA_MACRO_MCLK_FREQ;
+
 	if (!dmic_sample_rate || mclk_rate % dmic_sample_rate != 0)
 		goto undefined_rate;

